## Proof

The statement is **true**.

**Setup.** Since $f_n \to f$ in measure on $E$ with $m(E)<\infty$, every $f_n$ is measurable and $f$ is measurable (a subsequence converges to $f$ a.e.). Since $f:E\to\mathbb{R}$ is real-valued, $f$ is finite a.e.; with $m(E)<\infty$, continuity from above gives
$$\lim_{M\to\infty} m\big(\{x:|f(x)|>M\}\big)=0.$$

**Key estimate.** By the triangle inequality, $|f_n(x)|\le |f_n(x)-f(x)|+|f(x)|$, hence
$$|f_n(x)-f(x)|\cdot|f_n(x)|\le |f_n(x)-f(x)|\big(|f_n(x)-f(x)|+|f(x)|\big).$$

Fix $\eta>0$ and $\delta>0$. We will show $\limsup_{n\to\infty} m\{x:|f_n-f|\,|f_n|>\eta\}\le\delta$.

1. **Truncate $f$.** Choose $M>0$ so large that $m\{x:|f(x)|>M\}<\delta$.
2. **Choose the convergence threshold.** Pick $\epsilon>0$ small enough that $\epsilon^2+\epsilon M\le\eta$ (e.g. $\epsilon=\min\{1,\,\eta/(M+1)\}$).
3. **Define the bad set.** Let $A_n:=\{x:|f_n(x)-f(x)|>\epsilon\}$. Since $f_n\to f$ in measure, $m(A_n)\to 0$.

**Decomposition.** On the good region $G_n:=(E\setminus A_n)\cap\{x:|f(x)|\le M\}$ we have $|f_n-f|\le\epsilon$ and $|f|\le M$, so
$$|f_n-f|\cdot|f_n|\le \epsilon(\epsilon+M)=\epsilon^2+\epsilon M\le\eta.$$
Therefore $G_n$ does not intersect $\{x:|f_n-f|\,|f_n|>\eta\}$, and
$$\{x:|f_n-f|\,|f_n|>\eta\}\subseteq A_n\cup\{x:|f(x)|>M\}.$$

**Conclusion.** Taking measures,
$$m\{x:|f_n-f|\,|f_n|>\eta\}\le m(A_n)+m\{x:|f|>M\}.$$
Letting $n\to\infty$,
$$\limsup_{n\to\infty} m\{x:|f_n-f|\,|f_n|>\eta\}\le 0+\delta=\delta.$$
Since $\delta>0$ was arbitrary,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty} m\{x:|f_n(x)-f(x)|\,|f_n(x)|>\eta\}=0.$$

